A1. What husbands are when they talk in their sleep. ~ ASLEEP
A7. Nowadays the fellow who follows in his father's footsteps is apt to end up in this place. ~ CELLAR
A13. A Mexican flivver. ~ BURRO
A14. A crying need in the movies. ~ ONION
A16. A part of the verb "to be." ~ AM
A18. This has made more than one coal merchant rich. ~ COAL
A19. Something a loquacious woman does with her tongue. ~ USES
A20. This is what two is (abbr.). ~ CO
A21. What an old man does when he goes to the seashore. ~ LOOKS
A23. A little devil. ~ IMP
A25. A favorite number of the colored gentry. ~ SEVEN
A27. Min's lesser half. ~ ANDY
A28. A butcher's thumb rest. ~ SCALE
A29. What flappers like best next to themselves. ~ SILK
A30. Much fuss and bustle. ~ ADO
A32. A point of the compass (abbr.) ~ ESE
A34. Enervating Lemon Extract (abbr.). ~ ELE
A35. A mermaid's sheik. ~ MERMAN
A38. What girls are when they have that dreamy look in their eyes. ~ DROWSY
A40. Monkey business. ~ EVOLUTION
A41. All choking aside this is what a bum wrestler gets. ~ DOWNED
A44. Some is bad and some is verse. ~ POETRY
A48. This is crude stuff. ~ ORE
A49. A little of this would help a lot in life. ~ WIT
A51. To "shake a leg." ~ HIE
A52. Willingly. ~ LIEF
A54. East side flappers. ~ JANES
A55. A first of the month greeting. ~ BILL
A56. A person afflicted with a very dreadful disease. ~ LEPER
A58. Anything rare or precious. ~ GEM
A59. Twelve good men and true. ~ PANEL
A60. Nickname of King Alfred the Great. ~ AL
A61. A place famous for issuing Liberty bonds. ~ RENO
A63. This means "to braid." ~ PLAT
A65. To perform. ~ DO
A66. To trim with the beak. (Ask any chicken and don't confuse it with trimming the meek.) ~ PREEN
A67. A tax of one-tenth. ~ TITHE
A69. These are things all women follow. ~ STYLES
A70. Things that brokers give prospective investors that usually prove to be a lot of bull. ~ STEERS

D2. Sensible Bachelors (abbr.). ~ SB
D3. What all bachelors are. And we don't mean maybe! ~ LUCKY
D4. The god of love. ~ EROS
D5. An epoch. ~ ERA
D6. Gentlemen of leisure. ~ POLICE
D7. This is usually found in a parked car. ~ COUPLE
D8. Our old friends, the half measures of type. ~ ENS
D9. What the "light that lies in woman's eyes" does. ~ LIES
D10. What a man does when he starts an argument with his bitter half. ~ LOSES
D11. A very indefinite article. ~ AN
D12. A wifely greeting in the Orient. (Remember they don't have rolling-pins there!) ~ SALAAM
D15. A kind of business conducted by William Jennings Bryan. ~ MONKEY
D17. A French world. ~ MONDE
D20. Confining apartments. ~ CELLS
D22. A goat's herald. ~ ODOR
D24. The meeker sex. ~ MASCULINE
D26. The chief attraction at the seashore. ~ VIEW
D31. Cats have nine lives, but this bird croaks all the time. ~ RAVEN
D33. One who is sacrificed on the altar. ~ GROOM
D36. Meal ticket buyers. ~ MEN
D37. A dumb girl's yes. ~ NOD
D38. Slang for a pocket explorer. ("WIFE" is a four-letter word!) ~ DIP
D39. A lonesome number. ~ ONE
D41. George Washington made one of these go a long way. ~ DOLLAR
D42. A bay window. (This does not refer to any part of the human anatomy!) ~ ORIEL
D43. What women do to clinch an argument. ~ WEEP
D45. Something women are always trying to get. ~ THIN
D46. What pedestrians get when they try to solve a cross-road puzzle. ~ RILED
D47. A well-known color. ~ YELLOW
D49. Vehicles of periodical teetotalers. ~ WAGONS
D50. What a bargain sale does to a member of the weaker sex. ~ TEMPTS
D53. This is something that double crosses a river. ~ FERRY
D55. Some people do this once a month, whether they need to or not. ~ BATHE
D57. A drunkard's waltz. ~ REEL
D59. One of the round shiny things that decorate the first row of an orchestra. ~ PATE
D62. Born. ~ NEE
D64. What husbands and lamps get when they go out at night. ~ LIT
D66. Hip pocket size (abbr.). ~ PT
D68. Interjection of after-dinner speakers. ~ ER


